{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1610 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "970d1c66888e7b52d9d16b169a42d895db15aca24bf75e77f47c1c868579285e" :edb/db-before-t 1609 :edb/db-after-t 1610 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534922 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:05:31.645Z" 13194139534922 true] [17592186078220 1100 "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n5+7+9+\\cdots = 140\n$$" 13194139534922 true] [17592186078220 1100 "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series:\n$5+7+9+\\cdots = 140$" 13194139534922 false] [17592186078220 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\nS_{N} = \\dfrac{N}{2} \\big( 2a_1+(N-1)d \\big)\n$$\n\nWe are given the sum $S_N = 140,$ and the first term $a_1=5.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =7-5 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 140 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(5)+(N-1)(2)) \\\\ 140 & =\\frac{N}{2}(10+2N-2) \\\\ 140 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2N+8) \\\\ 140 & ={N}^{2}+4N \\\\ 0 & ={N}^{2}+4N-140.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{{4}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-140)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{576}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 24}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $N=10$ or $N=-14.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=10$ terms." 13194139534922 true] [17592186078220 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\nS_{N} = \\dfrac{N}{2} \\big(2a_1+(N-1)d \\big)\n$$\n\nWe are given the sum $S_N = 140$, and the first term $a_1=5$. Our goal is to find $N$, the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{2} - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 7 - 5 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= \\frac{N}{2}(2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) \\\\\n140 &= \\frac{N}{2}(2(5) + (N - 1)(2)) \\\\\n140 &= \\frac{N}{2}(10 + 2N - 2) \\\\\n140 &= \\frac{N}{2}(2N + 8) \\\\\n140 &= N^{2} + 4N \\\\\n0 &= N^{2} + 4N - 140\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN &= (-4 ± \\sqrt{4^{2} - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-140)})/(2 \\cdot 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-4 ± \\sqrt{576}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-4 ± 24}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $N=10$ or $N=-14$. We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=10$ terms." 13194139534922 false] [17592186078221 1100 "An arithmetic series has the $5$th term $a_{5}=14$, the $7$th term $a_{7}=19$, and the sum of the series is $S_N=850$. Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." 13194139534922 false] [17592186078221 1100 "An arithmetic series has the $5$th term $a_{5}=14,$ the $7$th term $a_{7}=19,$ and the sum of the series is $S_N=850.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." 13194139534922 true] [17592186078221 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac N 2 \\big( 2a_1 + (N-1) d \\big)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{7}-{a}_{5}}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{19-14}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $a_{5}=14.$ We can compute $a_1$ using the formula for the $n$th term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}+(5-1)\\cdot 2.5 \\\\ 14 & ={a}_{1}+4\\cdot 2.5 \\\\ 14 & ={a}_{1}+10 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, substituting $a_1=4,$ $d=2.5,$ and $S_N=850$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 850 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(4)+(N-1)(2.5)) \\\\ 850 & =\\frac{N}{2}(8+2.5N-2.5) \\\\ 850\\cdot 2 & =N(5.5+2.5N) \\\\ 1700 & =2.5{N}^{2}+5.5N \\\\ 2.5{N}^{2}+5.5N-1\\,700 & =0 \\\\ 5{N}^{2}+11N-3\\,400 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-(11)\\pm \\sqrt{(-11)^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-3400)}}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-11\\pm \\sqrt{68\\,121}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{-11\\pm 261}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $N=25$ or $N=-27.2.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=25$ terms." 13194139534922 true] [17592186078221 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac N 2 \\big(2a_1 + (N-1) d \\big)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{a_{7} - a_{5}}{7 - 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{19 - 14}{7 - 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2} \\\\\n&= 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $a_{5}=14$. We can compute $a_1$ using the formula for the $n$th term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\na_{5} &= a_{1} + (5 - 1) \\cdot 2.5 \\\\\n14 &= a_{1} + 4 \\cdot 2.5 \\\\\n14 &= a_{1} + 10 \\\\\na_{1} &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $a_1=4$, $d=2.5$, and $S_N=850$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= \\frac{N}{2}(2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) \\\\\n850 &= \\frac{N}{2}(2(4) + (N - 1)(2.5)) \\\\\n850 &= \\frac{N}{2}(8 + 2.5N - 2.5) \\\\\n850 \\cdot 2 &= N(5.5 + 2.5N) \\\\\n1700 &= 2.5N^{2} + 5.5N \\\\\n2.5N^{2} + 5.5N - 1700 &= 0 \\\\\n5N^{2} + 11N - 3400 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN &= (- (11) ± \\sqrt{(-11)^{2} - 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot (-3400)})/(2 \\cdot 5) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-11 ± \\sqrt{68121}}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-11 ± 261}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $N=25$ or $N=-27.2$. We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=25$ terms." 13194139534922 false] [17592186078222 1100 "The first term of an arithmetic series is $a_1=2$, the common difference is $d=4$, and the sum of the series is $S_N=200$. Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." 13194139534922 false] [17592186078222 1100 "The first term of an arithmetic series is $a_1=2,$ the common difference is $d=4,$ and the sum of the series is $S_N=200.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." 13194139534922 true] [17592186078222 1145 "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac N 2 \\big(2a_1 + (N-1) d \\big)\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a_1=2$, $d=4$, and $S_N=200$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= \\frac{N}{2}(2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) \\\\\n200 &= \\frac{N}{2}(2 \\cdot 2 + (N - 1) \\cdot 4) \\\\\n200 &= \\frac{N}{2}(4 + 4N - 4) \\\\\n200 &= \\frac{N}{2}(4N) \\\\\n200 &= 2N^{2} \\\\\n100 &= N^{2} \\\\\nN &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have taken the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative." 13194139534922 false] [17592186078222 1145 "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac N 2 \\big(2a_1 + (N-1) d \\big)\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a_1=2,$ $d=4,$ and $S_N=200$ into the above and solve for $N,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 200 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 2+(N-1)\\cdot 4) \\\\ 200 & =\\frac{N}{2}(4+4N-4) \\\\ 200 & =\\frac{N}{2}(4N) \\\\ 200 & =2{N}^{2} \\\\ 100 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have taken the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative." 13194139534922 true] [17592186078354 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320108 13194139534922 true] [17592186078354 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320115 13194139534922 true] [17592186078354 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320122 13194139534922 true] [17592186078354 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320178 13194139534922 true] [17592186078354 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320185 13194139534922 true] [17592186078355 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320129 13194139534922 true] [17592186078355 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320136 13194139534922 true] [17592186078355 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320143 13194139534922 true] [17592186078355 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320171 13194139534922 true] [17592186078356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320094 13194139534922 true] [17592186078356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320101 13194139534922 true] [17592186078356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320150 13194139534922 true] [17592186078356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320157 13194139534922 true] [17592186078356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320164 13194139534922 true] [17592186079035 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534922 true] [17592186079035 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nAn arithmetic series has the first term $a_{1} = 4$, the $8$th term $a_{8} = 60$, and the sum of the series is $S_{N} = 1600$. Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." 13194139534922 false] [17592186079035 1100 "An arithmetic series has the first term ${a}_{1}=4$, the $8$th term ${a}_{8}=60$, and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=1600.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." 13194139534922 true] [17592186079035 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{1}}{8-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{60-4}{8-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{7} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=4$, $d=8$, and ${S}_{N}=1600$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 1600 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 4+(N-1)\\cdot 8) \\\\ 1600 & =\\frac{N}{2}(8+8N-8) \\\\ 1600 & =\\frac{N}{2}(8N) \\\\ 1600 & =4{N}^{2} \\\\ 400 & ={N}^{2} \\\\ N & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative." 13194139534922 true] [17592186320094 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320095 13194139534922 true] [17592186320094 1089 "q-108898" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320094 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534922 true] [17592186320094 1100 "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=1,$ the common difference is $d=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=52.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." 13194139534922 true] [17592186320094 1113 #uuid "26800328-a78b-5992-a8a5-3714ffd4aa88" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320094 1145 "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d).\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${a}_{1}=1,$ $d=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${S}_{N}=52$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 52 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 1+(N-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 104 & =N(2+\\frac{N}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 104 & =\\frac{{N}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}N \\\\ {N}^{2}+3N-208 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-208)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{841}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm 29}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=13$ or $N=-16.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=13$ terms." 13194139534922 true] [17592186320095 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320096 13194139534922 true] [17592186320095 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320097 13194139534922 true] [17592186320095 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320098 13194139534922 true] [17592186320095 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320099 13194139534922 true] [17592186320095 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320100 13194139534922 true] [17592186320095 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534922 true] [17592186320095 1041 "selection" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320095 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320097 13194139534922 true] [17592186320095 1110 #uuid "7198a3cd-1df7-576d-afcf-4c9aa9ea3289" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320096 1034 "12" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320096 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534922 true] [17592186320096 1111 #uuid "7118e524-eade-539a-b842-cdf6cc529568" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320097 1034 "13" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320097 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534922 true] [17592186320097 1111 #uuid "c9115be1-0325-5557-984b-b560bfb34baa" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320098 1034 "11" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320098 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534922 true] [17592186320098 1111 #uuid "d6f196ed-5304-585c-8dde-fcbf1d27d35b" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320099 1034 "15" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320099 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534922 true] [17592186320099 1111 #uuid "bbfa87fd-3f36-56a8-81ea-a99dabe2a429" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320100 1034 "14" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320100 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534922 true] [17592186320100 1111 #uuid "51b7e4aa-43de-5055-b43f-63c355398670" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320101 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320102 13194139534922 true] [17592186320101 1089 "q-108900" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320101 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534922 true] [17592186320101 1100 "The first term of an arithmetic series is ${a}_{1}=5,$ the common difference is $d=-2.5,$ and the sum of the series is ${S}_{N}=-105.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." 13194139534922 true] [17592186320101 1113 #uuid "3f6251eb-1bcd-508b-8858-dbeab480b733" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320101 1145 "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d).\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${a}_{1}=5$, $d=-2.5$ and ${S}_{N}=-105$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ -105 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2\\cdot 5+(N-1)\\cdot (-2.5)) \\\\ -210 & =N(10-2.5N+2.5) \\\\ -210 & =12.5N-2.5{N}^{2} \\\\ 2.5{N}^{2}-12.5N-210 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-(-12.5)\\pm \\sqrt{(-12.5)^{2}-4\\cdot 2.5\\cdot (-210)}}{2\\cdot 2.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{12.5\\pm 47.5}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=12$ or $N=-7.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=12$ terms." 13194139534922 true] [17592186320102 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320103 13194139534922 true] [17592186320102 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320104 13194139534922 true] [17592186320102 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320105 13194139534922 true] [17592186320102 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320106 13194139534922 true] [17592186320102 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320107 13194139534922 true] [17592186320102 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534922 true] [17592186320102 1041 "selection" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320102 1104 #edb/ref 17592186320103 13194139534922 true] [17592186320102 1110 #uuid "86e3f0c6-b6ed-5913-ad69-8beca185d40c" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320103 1034 "12" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320103 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534922 true] [17592186320103 1111 #uuid "469992d5-e3fe-5d0a-be28-ef433115f2c1" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320104 1034 "10" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320104 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534922 true] [17592186320104 1111 #uuid "fa7390e8-5611-576f-bfcf-649aa0fc4ef0" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320105 1034 "16" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320105 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534922 true] [17592186320105 1111 #uuid "ca97d072-9e9a-50bc-bab9-ce6ce8d58b03" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320106 1034 "15" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320106 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534922 true] [17592186320106 1111 #uuid "5ecca16c-ae82-5b75-974d-8056364b7126" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320107 1034 "18" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320107 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534922 true] [17592186320107 1111 #uuid "61b8ca28-a0fe-593a-8070-79e186e861d1" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320108 1017 #edb/ref 17592186320109 13194139534922 true] [17592186320108 1089 "q-108902" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320108 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534922 true] [17592186320108 1100 "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n6+9+12+…=195\n$$" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320108 1113 #uuid "93ce8884-497d-55e7-a6f2-e2dbf44b2682" 13194139534922 true] [17592186320108 1145 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d)\\,\\,\n$$\n\nWe have been given the sum ${S}_{N}=195,$ and the first term ${a}_{1}=6.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =9-6 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 195 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(6)+(N-1)(3)) \\\\ 390 & =N(12+3N-3) \\\\ 390 & =N(9+3N) \\\\ 390 & =3{N}^{2}+9N \\\\ 3{N}^{2}+9N-390 & =0 \\\\ {N}^{2}+3N-130 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-130)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{529}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm 23}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $N=10$ or $N=-13.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=10$ terms." 13194139534922 true] [17592186320109 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320110 13194139534922 true] [17592186320109 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320111 13194139534922 true] [17592186320109 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320112 13194139534922 true] [17592186320109 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320113 13194139534922 true] [17592186320109 1000 #edb/ref 17592186320114 131941